#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0e5b503-a393-58ef-bbcd-5c6d65257f53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "bd57c7f2-87b5-55a0-9237-1ef814f59898" :question/math-academy-id "e-6136" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $2x < 10\\,$?\n\n1. $x=0$\n2. $x=2$\n3. $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 0 & < 10 \\\\ 0 & < 10✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $x=0$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & < 10 \\\\ 4 & < 10✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $x=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $x=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}5}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5 & < 10 \\\\ 10 & < 10\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $x=5$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. Therefore, $x=5$ **does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fdde75-121e-5378-bad1-1ab564375d41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Compound Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "74587421-d1ad-550e-a30d-4d4768588d4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6137" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $1 \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}-1 < 3\\,$?\n\n1. $x=0$\n2. $x=4$\n3. $x=10$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2} - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{0}{2} - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 0 - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le - 1 < 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. Therefore, $x=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}4}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2} - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{4}{2} - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2 - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 1 < 3✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $x=4$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}10}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2} - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{10}{2} - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 5 - 1 < 3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 4 < 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $x=10$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. Therefore, $x=10$ **does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd07be8d-2eba-5e4e-ba42-72470fd12120" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inequalities that are Satisfied by a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "fb564b00-2d04-556a-a662-7b92e21ec3d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6138" :question/problem "The value $a = 2$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{a}{2} + 2 > 5$\n2. $1 \\leq 2a < 6$\n3. $3a - 1 < 10$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the first inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{a}{2} + 2 & > 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{2} + 2 & > 5 \\\\ 1 + 2 & > 5 \\\\ 3 & > 5\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **false** statement. Therefore, $a = 2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the second inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\le 2a < 6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2 \\cdot a < 6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2 \\cdot 2 < 6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 4 < 6✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $a = 2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the third inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3a - 1 & < 10 \\\\ 3 \\cdot a - 1 & < 10 \\\\ 3 \\cdot 2 - 1 & < 10 \\\\ 5 & < 10✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $a = 2$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c472f18-da99-5969-acdf-1a7b53465494" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality with Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "73bc0b43-f3a9-5a4b-a49f-833cdf1f493f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6139" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{2t}{3} < 8\\,$?\n\n1. $t=9$\n2. $t=6$\n3. $t=3$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}9}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot 9}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & < 8 \\\\ 6 & < 8✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $t = 9$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $t=9$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot 6}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3} & < 8 \\\\ 4 & < 8✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $t=6$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $t=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & < 8 \\\\ 2 & < 8✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, substituting $t=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. Therefore, $t=3$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fdde75-121e-5378-bad1-1ab564375d41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Compound Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db1b76bd-cc4e-5494-bb2c-0ef5978d995e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067991} {:answer/id #uuid "eccebac7-1cc2-5ac8-8586-f1b4ba12efe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067992} {:answer/id #uuid "03eae620-0a72-5ac8-8c06-ed97d97ba78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067993} {:answer/id #uuid "0af28cfe-55f0-5316-a265-32fa7f9b5716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067994} {:answer/id #uuid "42d3994d-6cc2-58e9-94f1-8aa6404ab537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067995}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1045cfc3-db82-5ebf-9a58-ef4781fcdd16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067990}] :question/id #uuid "3cdbf09e-9c7d-57f8-ba2f-16618f332918" :question/math-academy-id "q-64151" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $a$ satisfy the inequality $-3 \\le 2a + 1 < 8$?\n\n1. $a =-2$\n2. $a = 2$\n3. $a = 5$"}]}
